# Known, already-public locations of denylisted values in this repo's
# history. scripts/check-pii.sh reports these as WARN instead of FAIL.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# A value that has reached a public repository cannot be un-published by a
# scanner. Before this file, `check-pii.sh --full` reported 130 FAILs --
# every one of them the same handful of values, in commits that have been
# on GitHub since 2026-08-18, none of them present at HEAD. A check that
# always fails is a check people stop reading, which is strictly worse than
# one that fails only on something new.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY EXPOSED
#
# Four values -- device identifiers (a MAC address and serials), not
# credentials, so there is nothing to revoke and nothing to authenticate
# with. Two files carry them: tests/test_assistant.py (since the initial
# commit, 2026-08-14) and tests/test_local_host.py (introduced 2026-09-12
# 07:56, scrubbed the same morning). Both are clean at HEAD; only the old
# commits still carry them.
#
# WHY THE HISTORY WAS NOT REWRITTEN
#
# It was attempted on 2026-09-12 and deliberately abandoned. `git
# filter-repo` produced a correct rewrite -- identical trees, all 135
# commits preserved, zero denylisted values remaining -- but this repo's
# ruleset requires signed commits, and rewriting strips every signature.
# Pushing it would have meant either re-signing 118 commits (putting the
# maintainer's signature on 19 dependabot[bot] commits, 3 PR merges, and 26
# commits previously signed by GitHub's web UI) or relaxing the signature
# rule and leaving those 118 commits unsigned forever. Neither was worth it
# for values of this kind. Worth knowing if it is ever reconsidered: a
# force-push would NOT remove the old commits from GitHub anyway -- they
# stay reachable by SHA until GitHub Support garbage-collects them.
#
# WHY THIS IS SAFE TO PUBLISH AND SAFE TO HAVE
#
# It holds locations, never values -- which is why, unlike .pii-denylist,
# it is tracked: CI and every clone then agree on the same exemptions.
# Each entry is an exact (commit sha, path) pair. A commit's sha derives
# from its content, so the same value appearing anywhere else -- in any
# commit made after this file was written -- has a different sha and still
# FAILs. This exempts the past; it cannot exempt the future.
#
# MAINTENANCE
#
# Do not add entries to silence a new finding: a new finding means the value
# has not been published yet, which is exactly the case worth blocking on.
# `--full` reports entries that match nothing, so prune those rather than
# letting the file rot into a blanket exemption.
#
# Format: <full commit sha> <path>
#
# --- tests/test_assistant.py: present since the initial commit ---
